Functional changes within PCBoard itself (ignoring Fido and PPL for a moment)
are minor due to effort put into porting PCBoard over to OS/2 and the desire to
"freeze" the feature set during much of the porting process.  However, a few
changes in operation have been made:

- A new option for the SHELL column in the DOORS.LST editor has been added.
  You can now define a door as type 'F', for "Fast Shell".

  When the Shell is set to "F", the door is shelled to *without* closing any
  files, without closing the comm port, without swapping out (under DOS), etc.
  It simply shells out and runs the door, then comes back as quickly as
  possible.

  NOTE:  It does create a users.sys file (if you tell it to), it also updates
  the pcboard.sys file.  It does NOT re-load all of PCBoard's files upon
  returning from a door the way a regular door operates, nor does it reopen
  any of the files that were open because they remain open during the shell.

- In a conference where Long TO Names are allowed, PCBoard will no longer
  allow you to change the subject when replying to a message unless you
  utilize the RO (reply other) command.  The reason for this change is for
  compliance with internet RFCs which require that the subject not be modified
  when replies are posted, except for the addition of "Re: " in front of the
  message (which the new UUOUT module will handle).

- In addition, PCBoard will now handle up to 120 characters for the subject.
  It does not allow you to enter a subject that long, but if a message from
  the internet has a subject that long it will be supported by PCBoard.

- A native version of PCBoard is now available, which includes:
  - An OS/2 text mode version of PCBoard (PCBOARD2.EXE)
  - A new program (a PM app) called the PCBoard Control Panel (PCBCP.EXE)
  - An OS/2 text mode version of PCBMoni (PCBMONI2.EXE)
  - An OS/2 text mode version of PCBPack (PCBPACK2.EXE)
  - An OS/2 text mode version of USERNET.EXE (USERNET2.EXE)

  If you already have PCBoard-for-DOS running in the OS/2 environment,
  setting up PCBoard-for-OS/2 will be a snap.  Most of your existing DOS
  programs will continue to run, unmodified, with PCBoard-for-OS/2.

- PCBSetup has been modified so that it now has an "OS/2" column in the
  EVENT.DAT editor and also in the DOORS.LST editor, to accomodate the running
  of OS/2 applications.  It also now has an "OS/2 Settings" screen in the
  Configuration Options menu for setting various OS/2 priority levels and
  controlling window behavior.

- PCBSM has been modified to speed up the "Pack Online" capability in
  situations where a large number of user records are in the users file, a
  large number of users need to be packed out, and the users at the very end of
  the file are not the ones that are to be packed out (basically, a scenario
  resulting in a "bad combination" for the design of the online packing
  process).  The change increases the possibility that the users file may not
  shrink (but only if the reason a record wasn't packed out or moved was
  because the user was online), but in exchange it greatly increases the
  packing speed by overcoming an exponential slowdown that occured in the
  previous design.

  Also, the "Pack Online" capability has been modified so that it will work on
  a system where networking is disabled.

  Normally you'd have no need to use the Pack Online feature in such a setting
  because it is normally used as a means of packing the users file while there
  is a possibility that users may be online.  However, a situation has arisen
  where it was needed for a different reason...  lack of disk space.

  The Pack Online operation does not use a "backup file" because it directly
  modifies the users and users.inf files.  So legitimately you may desire to
  use the Pack Online feature if you simply don't have enough room on your
  hard drive to use the normal pack method.

  Finally, the "Pack Online" capability has been modified so that it is no
  longer "disabled" if the alias support is enabled.  PCBoard is now storing
  the alias in a hidden field of the usernet record allowing PCBSM to check for
  the user being online even while PCBoard is hiding him from the WHO display.

- Added an option to PCBFiler, on Defaults Page 1, which controls whether or
  not PCBFiler will prompt on an overwrite when processing a DIR file.  The
  default is to ask (which is what it has always done), but you can change it
  to always assume yes (overwrite the file) or always assume no (never
  overwrite).


